{
 "id": "gap_abduce",
 "version": "1.0.0",
 "depends_on": [
  "time_axis"
 ],
 "description": "THE ENDPOINT-ABDUCED GENERATOR -- the ONE operator the timephi rung (93000-93999) measured the substrate to lack. Every other temporal mechanism here (TimeInfer, DepthInfer, BlinkBridge) infers its generator G from a CONSECUTIVE VISIBLE PAIR and EXTRAPOLATES: CONTINUATION. A phi stimulus supplies exactly ONE pre-gap sample, so there is no pair to continue from and BlinkBridge correctly FREEZES (confidence 0.00, resume correction 32 = the dot's 16 cells counted twice). This rule does the other thing: it solves G^(gap+1)(f_before) = f_after for G from the TWO ENDPOINTS ALONE -- INTERPOLATION. The admissibility test is DIVISIBILITY -- D[i] %% steps == 0 on every coordinate slot, exact integer arithmetic -- so PROTOCOL \u00a713 is satisfied BY CONSTRUCTION: not a number that falls out, but NO NUMBER AT ALL. SCOPE LIMIT, stated where the mechanism is: any two same-shape figures ARE rigid translates, so on the translate class divisibility is the ONLY constraint and the acceptance rate on independent random endpoints is ~1/steps, NOT zero -- interpolation is cheap for exactly the reason continuation is safe. Flag-gated on agent.gap_abduce_enabled==1, OFF by default (PROTOCOL \u00a79); inert without it AND matches only status=='gap_seq'.",
 "microtheory": {
  "name": "gap_abduce",
  "description": "a generator ABDUCED FROM THE TWO BANKS of a temporal gap rather than CONTINUED from a pair before it. Continuation needs two pre-gap samples and is CONSTRAINED by verification across every pair; interpolation needs only the endpoints and is constrained by nothing but divisibility. The substrate held the first and lacked the second."
 },
